anada's biggest  cities  especially young people and families  policy experts suggest  an urgent need to figure out and align  immigration policies with affordability  measures  we need to make sure that the way it  gets solved is  is both economically and environmentally  sustainable  ann gaviola global news toronto  who's protecting essential workers next  how canada has failed some of the most  critical people  in the pandemic  time and time again throughout this  pandemic we've been told  stay home to stay safe but for canadians  who don't have access to adequate sick  pay  the decision to stay home without pay  may not be a viable  option christa hessie on the calls for  more to be done  to keep workplaces safe this simple  memorial is a stark reminder of the toll  that kovat 19 has taken on essential  workers  since then like yo i've been asking that  question  what happened amanda young kaluchi's  brother godfrey young  was a long time employee at canada post  in mid-january he worked in night shift  at the gateway facility in mississauga  ontario  the 62 year old developed a cough then  tested positive for covet 19.  six days later he died the facility was  experiencing a covet outbreak  and there were close to 280 positive  cases  canada post declined global news's  request for an interview  and said in a statement that it has  implemented rigorous safety measures to  keep employees safe  and has closely followed the advice of  public health officials  including distancing and screening of  employees it says it also offers  paid sick leave essential worker like  him  have to dictate that type of risk every  day the question is  who's there to protect them workplaces  like food processing  warehousing and manufacturing continue  to be a key driver of transmission  in ontario alone 68 workers have died  after contracting covet at work  according to the province's workplace  safety and insurance board  more than 25 500 workers have been  infected at work  and 